9:## Individualized functional connectome abnormalities obtained using two normative model unveil neurophysiological subtypes of obsessive compulsive disorder
11:![Image](./Liu2024_artifacts/image_000001_9dcb3ab30fa3283148880f8fba2f8c20817e29fa2897d3476bd2fa28b0eb41e0.png)
13:Liang Liu a , Dongyao Jia a,* , Zihao He a , Baohong Wen b , Xiaopan Zhang b , Shaoqiang Han b,*
21:Keywords: Individualized analysis Functional connectivity strength Normative model OCD Subtype
25:Obsessive compulsive disorder (OCD) is a highly heterogeneous disorder. Patients with OCD show tremendous intersubject heterogeneity in symptom presentations and responses to treatment (Alexander et al., 1986; McKay et al., 2004). For example, cases with OCD might exhibit non-overlapping symptom profiles (Mataix-Cols et al., 2005). To address the heterogeneity, patients with OCD are often stratified into subtypes based on clinical symptoms (Mataix-Cols et al., 2004). Despite the great success, symptom-based subtyping has some limitations, such as no universally accepted taxonomy (McKay et al., 2004), low sensitivity and handing sub-threshold symptoms (Okada et al., 2015). Moreover, clinical symptoms interact with biological substrates in complex ways and may change over the course of the disease (Haas et al., 2024; Sun et al., 2023). Identifying neurophysiological subtypes of OCD could provide a more objective understanding of the disorder ' s
29:The high heterogeneity observed among patients with obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) underscores the need to identify neurophysiological OCD subtypes to facilitate personalized diagnosis and treatment. In this study, our aim was to identify potential OCD subtypes based on individualized functional connectome abnormalities. We recruited a total of 99 patients with OCD and 104 healthy controls (HCs) matched for demographic characteristics. Individualized functional connectome abnormalities were obtained using normative models of functional connectivity strength (FCS) and used as features to unveil OCD subtypes. Sensitivity analyses were conducted to assess the reproducibility and robustness of the clustering outcomes. Patients exhibited significant intersubject heterogeneity in individualized functional connectome abnormalities. Two subtypes with distinct patterns of FCS abnormalities relative to HCs were identified. Subtype 1 patients primarily exhibited significantly decreased FCS in regions including the frontal gyrus, insula, hippocampus, and precentral/postcentral gyrus, whereas subtype 2 patients demonstrated increased FCS in widespread brain regions. When all patients were combined, no significant differences were observed. Additionally, the identified subtypes showed significant differences in age of onset. Furthermore, sensitivity analyses confirmed the reproducibility of our subtyping results. In conclusion, the identified OCD subtypes shed new light on the taxonomy and neurophysiological heterogeneity of OCD.
31:biological mechanisms and improve personalized diagnosis and treatment. Yet, it remains challenging to identify such subtypes. In recent years, researchers have increasingly turned to objective neuroimaging data to address this challenge in mental disorders (Beijers et al., 2019; Zhang et al., 2021). However, there is a paucity of relevant researches in OCD.
33:Using modern neuroimaging methods like resting-state functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI), researchers have consistently linked the etiology of psychiatric disorders, including OCD, to imbalances in large-scale interconnected brain networks (Fornito et al., 2015; Han et al., 2023b). Patients with OCD often exhibit altered functional connections among brain systems and disrupted topological organization of functional connectomes (Geffen et al., 2022; Lei et al., 2017). However, findings in this area are inconsistent due to the tremendous intersubject heterogeneity of functional connectomes in patients with OCD (Lv et al., 2021b; Shan et al., 2019). Intersubject heterogeneity
41:![Image](./Liu2024_artifacts/image_000002_d38d6de4bb72c424f3a2d506d67f4b3c088f1815eca088a2be7d5ae933444563.png)
43:![Image](./Liu2024_artifacts/image_000003_18d23b842c87297338aabaf9f680bfcfd8e0ff08dc248753e2de5a299208a094.png)
47:In this study, we conducted a comprehensive investigation into the neurobiological heterogeneity and subtypes of OCD using a dataset comprising 99 patients with OCD and 104 healthy controls. We obtained individualized functional connectome abnormalities, measured with functional connectivity strength (FCS), using the normative model framework. Subsequently, we identified potential subtypes based on individualized FCS abnormalities as features. Finally, we evaluated differences in clinical variables and functional connectomes between the identified OCD subtypes.
55:Atotal of ninety-eight first-episode and untreated patients diagnosed with OCD were recruited from outpatient services within the Department of Psychiatry at the First Affiliated Hospital of Zhengzhou University. Diagnosis was established independently by two experienced psychiatrists in accordance with the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fifth Edition (DSM-5) criteria for OCD. The severity of symptoms was assessed using the Yale -Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale (Y-BOCS) (Goodman et al., 1989). Exclusion criteria ensured that none of the patients had a history of neurological disorders, brain trauma, concurrent mental or psychotic disorders, or first-degree relatives with similar conditions.
79:## L. Liu et al.
83:## 2.6. Identifying OCD subtypes based on individualized functional connectivity strength abnormalities
85:We employed a data-driven k-means clustering algorithm to investigate potential subtypes within OCD. In this approach, individualized FCS abnormalities served as the clustering feature, with the squared Euclidean distance utilized as the distance metric. To determine the optimal number of subtypes, ranging from 2 to 10, we utilized silhouette values (Han et al., 2022b). For each candidate number of subtypes, the k-means algorithm was iterated 100 times to mitigate the risk of converging to local minima during the initialization of centroid positions (Allen et al., 2014).
93:A series of sensitivity analyses were conducted to ensure the robustness and reproducibility of the clustering outcomes. Firstly, to evaluate the stability of our results and minimize the influence of small subsets of patients, we randomly selected 90% of the patients and performed k-means clustering on this subsample, following the same procedure as before. Subsequently, we calculated the Adjusted Rand Index (ARI) between the subtyping results obtained from this subsample and those derived from the entire patient cohort. This process was repeated 1000 times to obtain a comprehensive assessment of the clustering consistency. Secondly, we validated our primary findings by employing another brain atlas with different resolutions, specifically the Automated Anatomical Labeling (AAL) atlas. The AAL atlas contains 90 cortical and subcortical regions (excluding the cerebellum) (TzourioMazoyer et al., 2002). Considering the important role of the cerebellum in OCD, we also validated our main clustering results using all brain regions in the AAL (including the cerebellum)(Xu et al., 2019; Zhang et al., 2019b). This validation step ensured that our results were not dependent on the specific brain parcellation scheme used in the analysis, thus enhancing the generalizability of our findings.
